 "name": "Pocket Park Planting: Coherence, Legibility, Refuge",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: five vacant plots in Tehran (object focused, one side, two sides, two sides between buildings, three sides protected) are each shown in five photomontaged planting designs that vary visual and physical enclosure, following Robinson's permeability of enclosure. For each plot, participants choose the design that looks most coherent, most legible and offers the best refuge. The 25 montages are those printed in Fig. 8 of Naghibi, Faizi & Ekhlassi (2022), MethodsX 9: 101638 (CC BY 4.0). Answers store the paper's scene number whatever position it was shown in. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper names the three criteria and says participants chose the favourite scene, but does not print the questions, so the question wording and the short definitions are ours; (2) the paper's reported analysis (mean ranks and medians per plot, Table 6) cannot be reproduced exactly from its description; (3) plot order and design order are random; (4) the paper's socio-demographic, park-use and activity sections are reduced to age, gender, a professional-background question and a park-visit question (our wording); (5) images are the small versions printed in the figure (about 250 px wide).",
